23:04:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're thinking of the ideal, and you're right that in a perfect world, an update would instantly and universally land on every device—like turning on a faucet and everyone gets water at the exact same time. Things would feel broken if you saw half your friends get a new game feature and you didn't. But in practice, the update system deliberately **does not** hit every device at once, and it is designed to handle exceptions gracefully, or else it *would* be broken. Here’s why: **1. Staged Rollouts (the biggest “exception”)** Developers almost never push an update to 100% of devices on day one. They release it to 1%, then 5%, then 20%, watching for crashes or bugs. If something goes wrong, they can halt it before it bricks everyone’s phone. So for a while, your device might not get it while your neighbor’s does—and that’s intentional, not broken. **2. Device Incompatibility** Many updates are simply not available for certain devices. An app might drop support for an older OS version (e.g., needs iOS 17, so iPhone X stuck on iOS 16 gets nothing). Or a game update requiring a newer graphics card won't be delivered to a PC that can't run it. The “update” never arrives for those devices—the system filters them out. **3. User Choice & Settings** Some people turn off automatic updates. Some keep their device permanently offline. Others never launch the app again. The update is sitting on the server, but those devices never ask for it. That’s not broken; it’s the user opting out. **4. Region & Distribution Quirks** A small number of updates are region-specific due to legal or content rules. Your device in one country might get it later, or not at all. **5. Hardware Limitations** Console players with full hard drives might not have space to download the update, so it won’t install until they free up room. The update is “available” but doesn’t physically land. So, does every device with the app installed get the update? **In theory, yes, eventually, if the device is compatible, online, and configured to accept it.** But in the real world, exceptions are not only common—they’re part of the safety net that keeps the whole system from collapsing. The system is working precisely because it **doesn’t** blindly force-feed the update to every single device with no exceptions.
